arty Jackley's decisive primary win and roughly twelve-to-one cash advantage, and markets pricing the GOP near ninety-four percent outweigh a Democratic-friendly environment and a single late-August poll showing a four-point race.", "watch": "A second poll confirming a single-digit race or new Democratic outside spending would signal this is tightening beyond the district's baseline.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No expert rater lists this race; treated as safe for the party holding the seat.", "Newest usable poll is 48 days old; polling uncertainty inflated.", "Open seat: Dusty Johnson (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "South Dakota's at-large House seat is open after Republican Dusty Johnson chose to run for governor, but the district's fifteen-point Republican lean keeps it firmly in the party's column. Marty Jackley, a former state attorney general who won a contested primary, holds a large financial edge with about $1.2 million on hand. Democrat Nikki Gronli has raised roughly $387,000, with about $93,000 in cash remaining, and no outside groups have spent in the race on either side.\n\nA late-August poll sponsored by a Democratic firm showed Jackley up four points among likely voters, a narrower gap than the district's partisan baseline would suggest. Prediction markets still price the Republican at about ninety-four percent. The combination of a strong partisan lean, a well-known and well-funded nominee, and minimal Democratic investment points to a Republican hold, softened somewhat by a national environment that has favored Democrats this cycle.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0322, "margin": -15.31}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.035, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0242, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0439, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0257, "margin": -15.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.0188, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.0357, "m